163| \begin{definition}[Radical clock]\label{def:b3:radicals-carbenes:radical-clock}
164| A \emph{radical clock}\index{radical clock} is a radical that rearranges
165| unimolecularly with a known rate constant, such as the 5-hexenyl radical
166| cyclising; its competition with a bimolecular trap measures the rate constant
167| of the trap, or the reverse.
168| \end{definition}
169| 
170| \begin{proposition}[Competition kinetics]\label{prop:b3:radicals-carbenes:clock}
171| If a radical either cyclises ($k_c$) or is trapped by a hydride kept in excess at
172| concentration $[\mathrm{R_3SnH}]$ ($k_H$), the product ratio is
173| $[\text{cyclised}]/[\text{direct}] = k_c/(k_H[\mathrm{R_3SnH}])$.
174| \end{proposition}
175| 
176| \begin{proof}
177| At every moment the two products form at rates $k_c[\mathrm R^\bullet]$ and $k_H[\mathrm{R_3SnH}][\mathrm R^\bullet]$. Their ratio
178| does not depend on the radical concentration, which varies; with the hydride
179| concentration constant, integration over time multiplies both by the same
180| $\int[\mathrm R^\bullet]\,\dd t$, and the ratio of the products is the ratio of the rate constants
181| times $1/[\mathrm{R_3SnH}]$.
182| \end{proof}
